# PHP/HTML Templating Standards

## Data Collection and Organization

Always collect all data at the top of PHP/HTML files for clarity and maintainability. Whenever possible, retrieve data from the component class rather than calculating values in the template.

### ✅ Correct Approach

```php
<?php
// At the top of the file - collect all data
$title = $this->getTitle();
$description = $this->getDescription();
$items = $this->getItems();
$imageId = $this->getFeaturedImageId();
$imageUrl = $imageId ? wp_get_attachment_image_url($imageId, 'large') : '';
$imageAlt = $imageId ? \TechnoSapiens\Core\Image::altFromId($imageId) : esc_attr($title);
?>

<div class="component">
    <h2 class="component__title"><?php echo esc_html($title); ?></h2>
    <p class="component__description"><?php echo esc_html($description); ?></p>

    <?php if ($imageUrl) : ?>
        <img src="<?php echo esc_url($imageUrl); ?>" alt="<?php echo esc_attr($imageAlt); ?>">
    <?php endif; ?>

    <?php if (!empty($items)) : ?>
        <ul class="component__list">
            <?php foreach ($items as $item) : ?>
                <li><?php echo esc_html($item); ?></li>
            <?php endforeach; ?>
        </ul>
    <?php endif; ?>
</div>
```

### ❌ Incorrect Approach

```php
<div class="component">
    <h2 class="component__title"><?php echo esc_html($this->getTitle()); ?></h2>
    <p class="component__description"><?php echo esc_html($this->getDescription()); ?></p>

    <?php
    $imageId = $this->getFeaturedImageId();
    $imageUrl = $imageId ? wp_get_attachment_image_url($imageId, 'large') : '';
    if ($imageUrl) : ?>
        <img src="<?php echo esc_url($imageUrl); ?>" alt="<?php echo esc_attr($this->getTitle()); ?>">
    <?php endif; ?>

    <?php
    $items = $this->getItems();
    if (!empty($items)) : ?>
        <ul class="component__list">
            <?php foreach ($items as $item) : ?>
                <li><?php echo esc_html($item); ?></li>
            <?php endforeach; ?>
        </ul>
    <?php endif; ?>
</div>
```

## Use English for Function Names and Placeholders

Always use English for function names, variable names, and any placeholder text in the code. This ensures consistency and readability across the codebase.

### ✅ Correct

```php
function getProductCategories() {
    // Implementation
}

$placeholderText = 'Enter your search term';
```

### ❌ Incorrect

```php
function haal_product_categorien() {
    // Implementation
}

$placeholderTextDutch = 'Voer je zoekterm in';
```

## Proper Template Syntax

Always use the proper PHP/HTML templating approach when writing theme files. Never echo large blocks of HTML from PHP.

### ✅ Correct Approach

```php
<?php if ($condition) : ?>
    <div class="element">
        <h2><?php echo esc_html($title); ?></h2>
        <p><?php echo esc_html($content); ?></p>
    </div>
<?php endif; ?>
```

### ❌ Incorrect Approach

```php
<?php
if ($condition) {
    echo '<div class="element">';
    echo '<h2>' . esc_html($title) . '</h2>';
    echo '<p>' . esc_html($content) . '</p>';
    echo '</div>';
}
?>
```

## PHP Control Structures in Templates

Use the alternative syntax (:, endforeach, endif) for control structures.

### ✅ Correct

```php
<?php foreach ($items as $item) : ?>
    <li><?php echo esc_html($item); ?></li>
<?php endforeach; ?>

<?php if ($condition) : ?>
    <?php elseif ($otherCondition) : ?>
    <?php else : ?>
    <?php endif; ?>
```

### ❌ Incorrect

```php
<?php foreach ($items as $item) { ?>
    <li><?php echo esc_html($item); ?></li>
<?php } ?>

<?php if ($condition) { ?>
    <?php } elseif ($otherCondition) { ?>
    <?php } else { ?>
    <?php } ?>
```

## Output Escaping (VERY IMPORTANT!)

**⚠️ IMPORTANT: These escaping rules apply primarily to NEW code. When editing existing, working code, do not add escaping functions unless there is a genuine security vulnerability or the user explicitly requests it.**

Protecting against XSS is vital. The type of escaping depends on the source and context. When in doubt, always escape!

**Key Principles for NEW Code:**

1.  **HTML Attributes:** ALWAYS use `esc_attr()` when outputting any variable into an HTML attribute (e.g., `class`, `id`, `alt`, `title`, `data-*`).
2.  **URLs:** ALWAYS use `esc_url()` when outputting any variable into a URL context (e.g., `href`, `src`, `action`).
3.  **HTML Content (between tags like `<p>`, `<h1>`):**
    *   **User Input / Untrusted Data:** ALWAYS use `esc_html()`. This includes `$_GET`, `$_POST`, search terms, etc.
    *   **ACF WYSIWYG:** ALWAYS use `wp_kses_post()` (or a trusted wrapper like `Formatting::toHtml` if it correctly implements `wp_kses_post` or similar filtering) to allow safe HTML.
    *   **ACF Simple Text/Textarea / WP Core Data (like `get_the_title`):** While often pre-sanitized, the safest default practice is to use `esc_html()`. Only omit `esc_html()` if you are absolutely certain the data is safe and you intentionally need raw (but non-HTML) output. It's generally better to be safe.
4.  **Inline JavaScript:** AVOID outputting PHP into JS. Use `wp_localize_script` or data attributes. If absolutely necessary (and rare), use `esc_js()`.

**When Modifying Existing Code:**
- Only add escaping if there is a genuine security vulnerability
- Do not modify existing escaping patterns unless they are causing errors
- Respect the existing code style and patterns

